from addiction to a devastating medical  diagnose shelly keller grew up with a  lot of obstacles  but she's a shining example of how the  biggest challenges can become  opportunities to create your own life  and the lives of others  she's inspired countless students to  achieve their dreams and as alex backus  shows us that's just one reason she was  selected as a remarkable woman  dr shelly keller has spent 15 years at  the college of southern nevada  it's a place she never imagined she  could be  i walked into that campus and it changed  my life  shelly was born in ohio she experienced  trauma as a child  and remembers having her first drink at  six years old  just a sip you know when i tasted it i  immediately  thought oh this this helps me feel  better by the time she was 13  shelley was drinking heavily she was  also having major stomach issues  had surgery then received devastating  news  they're telling me i can't have children  and that devastated me  shelley dropped out of high school  continued drinking and became  addicted to meth for 15 years that was  the hardest thing i ever did  was to stop drinking i  i went to that rehab but that wasn't the  first rehab i went to  she kept fighting in 2006 shelley was  figuring out her next steps  and a counselor asked if she thought  about community college  shelley walked onto the csn campus and  with guidance  and encouragement enrolled as a student  it gave me hope  it gave me self-worth it connected me to  people that  lived different ways shelley went on to  unlv where she's earned a phd  she is the first in her family to  graduate college  but shelley wanted to return to csn she  was hired as a public speaking professor  she is now the director for the centers  for academic success  her job is helping students achieve  their dreams  it's the most remarkable experience to  tell you the truth is to  be giving back barbara iarza's dream was  college  at 40 years old a single mom of six and  a high school dropout  her dream seemed out of reach been  through about the worst of the worst  so homeless at a woman's shelter  lost both my parents she went to student  orientation at csn  and met dr shelly keller her first day  basically just  bawled my eyes out and told her my story  and how desperate i was  and having no idea that she was the  perfect person  in the perfect position shelly helped  barbara with a job  child care and most importantly  encouragement  barbara since landed a full ride to unlv  where she's earned a bachelor's  in psychology she's just really  everything to me  she really is because i would never hate  it  thousands of students that can tell you  that how she's touched their lives  while she succeeded professionally  shelley still dreamt of having her own  family  she and her husband became foster  parents and 14 years ago  they adopted their two children emma and  joseph  both were impacted by drugs and alcohol  and i knew  that i would have something to offer  those kids  they are the best kids they're just  they're remarkable  if you went back to being 13  and could see you right now  would you ever believe that that  question will make me cry  because no no i was so  uh i had lost hope  as a child shelly has been sober since  2010  and her mission to help others is far  from over some of the hardest things you  go through  in the end you see so much meaning if  you just look for it  alex backus 8 news now  shelley credits counseling for helping  her turn her life around  and she returned to school to become a  counselor herself christian well she  also started a non-profit the nevada  institute for mental health  and addiction great story
 

